    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1977449784819777570",
      "url": "https://x.com/janwriter_/status/1977449784819777570",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/janwriter_/status/1977449784819777570",
      "text": "Congratulations to everyone who showed up today in Forbes Park.\n\nLet me just share a widely studied principle in political science called the “3.5% rule.”\n\nAccording to research, history shows that when around 3.5% of a nation’s population consistently participates in protests, that movement almost always succeeds in forcing real change.\n\nFor the Philippines, that’s roughly 4 million people.\n\nBut here’s the key part: they don’t have to protest all at once or in one place. That number refers to collective and sustained participation — people across the country taking part in waves, over weeks or months, through rallies, strikes, organizing, and civic actions.\n\nIt’s about building steady pressure.\n\nSo today matters. Every action adds up. Every gathering contributes to that momentum. What counts is not just the size of one protest, but the persistence of many.\n\nAnd as the country’s situation grows more fragile by the day — especially with the impending economic winter — I can only see these protests gaining strength in the coming weeks and months.",
